### [x] REC-01 — Correct the installation and snapshot recovery runbook

Priority: P1. Type: confirmed documentation inconsistencies; recovery untested.
Files: `docs/install.md`, `modules/btrfs.nix`, host hardware file (read-only).

Evidence: the runbook hardcodes `/dev/nvme0n1`, assumes backups already exist,
and instructs manual UUID changes after generating hardware config. It presents
`sudo snapper -c root rollback <number>` as data rollback for `/etc`, `/var`, and
`/home`, but `/home` is a separate subvolume and root is mounted explicitly with
`subvol=@`. Pre/post examples do not pass the pre-snapshot number to the post
snapshot. `modules/btrfs.nix` also points to the wrong runbook step number.

Implementation:
1. Separate install, NixOS-generation rollback, root-data restore, home-data
   restore, and external-media restore into explicit procedures.
2. Require target identity (model/serial/size/mounts), current backup proof, and
   a reviewed destructive command before formatting. Do not add runnable wipe
   automation. Treat existing device paths as examples, not discovery.
3. Document copying freshly generated hardware configuration without hand-editing
   generated UUIDs. Put intentional policy overrides in separate modules.
4. Validate how Snapper recovery works with the explicit `@`/`@home` layout in
   a disposable VM or spare filesystem. Do not assume changing the default
   Btrfs subvolume changes a boot configured with `subvol=@`.
5. Fix paired snapshots using the returned pre-snapshot number. Explain that
   snapshots on the same disk are not independent backups and do not cover
   `/media` or provide automatic database-consistent exports.
6. Clarify first-login password/SSH setup before losing installer access.

Done when: the runbook describes separately verified recovery for each data
scope, includes explicit preconditions, and no step claims a root snapshot
restores the separate home/media filesystems. Never test destructive steps live.

Status: completed as documentation and disposable-VM validated; full host boot
rollback has not been tested. The runbook records this limit explicitly.
Commit(s): `c9badf6` (`Correct installation and recovery runbook`).
Changed files: `docs/install.md`, `modules/btrfs.nix`, and this task record.
Checks run and exact outcomes: `git diff --check` passed and
`nix-instantiate --parse modules/btrfs.nix` passed. A disposable NixOS VM with
an empty virtual disk created the `@`, `@home`, and standalone `.snapshots`
subvolumes. Snapper snapshot creation succeeded; rollback at the custom-mounted
`/mnt/root-layout` was rejected as a non-root subvolume. The explicit mount
remained `subvol=@` while Btrfs default remained `@home`. Snapper `create`
help confirmed `-p` prints the pre-snapshot number and `--pre-number` pairs the
post snapshot. Independent review by `work01_review` passed; its suggested
distinct range placeholders were applied.
Operational checks not run and why: no host disk was formatted, mounted, or
modified. Full recovery/boot rollback must still be tested on a disposable
system that reproduces the host's root mount at `/` before being relied on.
Remaining prerequisites or operator action: none for the documentation task;
do not treat snapshots on the host disk as external backups.

### [x] THEME-01 — Fix theme-picker cleanup and concurrent activation

Priority: P1. Type: confirmed cleanup defect plus race risk.
Files: `home/desktop/sway/scripts/theme-picker`, `theme-preview`.

Evidence: `theme-picker` installs `trap cleanup` around line 38, then replaces
it with a temporary-file-only trap around line 84. Accepting a selection can
skip closing the preview and deleting its state file. Each selection also
launches a new background Home Manager activation against a mutable repo and
writes the same activation log.

Implementation:
1. Keep one cleanup function. Initialize both temporary paths, remove each if
   set, close only this invocation's preview, and preserve the exit status.
2. Create the replacement preferences file beside `home/variables.nix`, preserve
   its intended mode, then rename it atomically. Do not rely on cross-filesystem
   `mv` from `/tmp` for atomicity.
3. Serialize selection writes and Home Manager activation, or coalesce pending
   changes so the latest accepted selection wins. Coordinate with BUILD-01;
   do not invent a second incompatible activation lock.
4. Use a per-run log and report whether persistence/activation succeeded. On
   failure, make the difference between live preview and persisted selection
   explicit; do not silently revert a newer selection.

Done when: stubbed tests cover cancel, success, preview failure, activation
failure, and two rapid invocations; no preview process/state file leaks and an
older activation cannot overwrite the newer selection.

Completed: picker cleanup is a single status-preserving trap; preview startup
reports Kitty failures and close targets the unique preview title. Theme
preferences are atomically replaced beside the source file with its mode
preserved. Picker sessions serialize writes, while activations use BUILD-01's
shared lock and per-run logs. Stub fixtures passed for cancel, success, preview
failure, activation failure, rapid selections, and startup/cleanup failures.
Independent review passed. Flake check and the Home Manager activation build
passed; the latter used a disposable source snapshot because the checkout has
an unrelated untracked Fish file. No live activation was run. Commit: `7359000`.

### [ ] POWER-01 — Make the fan reapply timer actually run again

Priority: P1. Type: confirmed systemd semantic mismatch.
Files: `modules/letsnote/default.nix`, `modules/letsnote/README.md`.

Evidence: `letsnote-fan-eco` is a oneshot with `RemainAfterExit = true`, while
`letsnote-fan-eco-reapply` targets that same service at 45 and 180 seconds. The
installed `systemd.timer` manual says an already-active target service is not
restarted by a timer. Live service was active/exited since its first boot run.
The comment says quiet byte `0x05`, while the command sends `0x01`.

Implementation:
1. Make the repeatable command a oneshot that becomes inactive after completion,
   or add a separate repeatable service. Preserve the intended boot ordering.
2. Resolve the 0x05/0x01 discrepancy against the existing verified hardware
   evidence; do not change an EC value based only on its comment.
3. Inspect and report the ACPI call result instead of equating a successful
   write to `/proc/acpi/call` with firmware acceptance.
4. Update stale panafanpwr/unsupported-model descriptions to match the actual
   acpi_call implementation, without widening supported hardware claims.

Done when: a harmless stub service demonstrates initial plus scheduled runs,
unit state no longer prevents repetition, and hardware changes are separately
reviewed and verified on the correct model. Keep the configured EC byte
separate from the source-level timer fix; verify the applied fan profile on the
CF-FV1 before claiming hardware behavior.

Status: timer and result-reporting fixes are built and fixture-verified. The
service is now an inactive-after-exit oneshot, so both scheduled starts can run.
The existing CF-FV1 request remains `0x01`; the stale `0x05` comment referred to
CF-SV1 and was corrected without changing the hardware request. Documentation
now distinguishes this EC quiet-profile request from general fan-speed control
and `panafanpwr`, which is not used. The actual EC response was not queried on
the laptop during this change, so hardware behavior remains unverified here.
Changed files: `modules/letsnote/default.nix`, `modules/letsnote/power.nix`,
`modules/letsnote/README.md`, `hosts/cf-fv1/laptop.nix`, `docs/install.md`,
the repository `README.md`, and this record.
Checks: flake check and NixOS build passed. `systemd-analyze verify` accepted
the generated service and timer; the generated service omits
`RemainAfterExit`, and the timer has 45-second and 180-second `OnBootSec`
entries. A disposable user-systemd oneshot/timer fixture ran five times in
0.8 seconds, confirming repeated starts. ACPI-result fixtures accepted `0x0`
and rejected `Error: AE_NOT_FOUND` and `not called`. No ACPI call was sent to
the live EC. Independent review confirmed service/timer semantics and found
two stale documentation claims; those were corrected and follow-up review
passed.
Remaining verification: check the method result and actual quiet-profile
behavior on the CF-FV1 before marking POWER-01 complete.
Implementation commit: `260c8a9` (`Make fan reapply timer repeat`).

Read-only live check (2026-09-27): the unit ran three times in this boot,
including two recent timer activations; each exited successfully and logged an
ACPI response of `0xffff0500`. The active invocation was inactive after exit,
and no system units were failed. Available fan RPM sensors report `N/A`; CPU
package temperature was 53 C during the check. This confirms repeated service
execution and a non-error method response, but does not prove the EC applied
the requested profile or that its fan behavior is correct. No EC write or
stress test was issued. POWER-01 remains open for a safe behavior check.

Read-only recheck (2026-09-28): the service remains inactive after a successful
exit. Its timer is enabled but `active (elapsed)` with no next trigger. The
journal contains two further successful service runs, at 20:33 and 21:50, each
logging `0xffff0500` alongside Bash's ignored-NUL-byte warning. Current readable
temperature sensors ranged from 39 C to 43 C; no fan RPM value was available.
Unprivileged access to `/proc/acpi/call` is denied. These observations neither
identify the response encoding nor prove the EC applied the quiet profile, so
POWER-01 remains pending the hardware behavior check.

Read-only recheck after reboot (2026-09-29): the oneshot again completed three
times (23:13:17, 23:13:55, and 23:16:11), each logging response
`0xffff0500` and Bash's ignored-NUL-byte warning. The service is inactive after
success; the timer is active (elapsed) with no further trigger configured.
Readable temperatures ranged from about 39 C to 46 C. This confirms repeated
post-boot invocation, but the response meaning and actual fan-curve behavior
remain unverified; no EC write or stress test was issued.

Result-log follow-up (2026-09-29): a NUL-terminated synthetic response
reproduced both the Bash warning and literal `\\n` in the old journal message.
The script now strips the ACPI result's NUL terminator and emits an actual
newline; the built unit contains the corrected command. The same synthetic
response check passes, as do Nix parsing, the full flake check, and the NixOS
build. No ACPI call was sent to the live EC. The value `0xffff0500` remains
unclassified, and quiet-profile behavior is still pending hardware review.
The upstream `acpi_call` implementation formats integer ACPI return objects in
hex and reports ACPI lookup/evaluation failures as `Error: ...`; therefore the
observed value is an integer method result, not that module's generic error
text. Its FV1-specific meaning and whether the EC applied the requested profile
remain unknown. Source: <https://github.com/nix-community/acpi_call/blob/master/acpi_call.c>.

Read-only firmware inspection (2026-09-29): DMI identifies the machine as
`CFFV1-1`, but its DSDT/SSDT files are mode 0400 and owned by root; `iasl` and
`acpidump` are not installed. No privileged command or EC operation was used,
so this session could not inspect the model's AML or determine the firmware
meaning of the method result.

### [x] BUILD-01 — Make system/Home Manager activation recoverable and serialized

Priority: P1. Type: reliability gap.
Files: `home/shell/default.nix` (`gnesha-rebuild`),
`hosts/cf-fv1/services.nix` (`gnesha-update-apply`), theme picker.

Evidence: rebuild freezes and builds both outputs, but switches NixOS then
Home Manager without a durable transaction record. Its temporary build links
are deleted on exit even when the second activation fails. The updater has its
own flock, while normal rebuild/theme activation do not share that lock.

Implementation:
1. Record the input snapshot, old/new system and home generations, and activation
   phase in a private state directory before switching anything.
2. Use a shared activation lock across rebuild, update-apply, and background
   theme activation. Keep long background builds outside that lock where safe.
3. Keep candidate GC roots and logs after failure until the operator resumes or
   explicitly discards the attempt. Set a bounded retention policy.
4. Distinguish build failure, system activation failure, and home activation
   failure in exit messages. Provide an explicit resume/recovery command.
5. Do not promise atomic two-layer activation or automatically roll back live
   databases/services. Preserve the current single-source-snapshot behavior.

Done when: injected failures at each phase leave inspectable state, a safe retry
uses the same validated closures, and concurrent activation attempts cannot
interleave. Use stubbed `nh`/Nix commands; do not fault-inject on the laptop.

Completed: shared activation state, bounded retention, exact-closure resume,
and locking are implemented. Stubbed failure/retry and lock-contention scenarios
passed; a stale-source fixture confirmed rebuild refuses to switch after another
change and retains the attempt. Independent review passed. `nix flake check`,
the NixOS build, and the Home Manager activation build passed; the latter used a
disposable source snapshot because the checkout contains an unrelated untracked
Fish file. No live activation was run. Commit: `7b69611`.

Current-machine recovery state (read-only, 2026-09-29): `gnesha-rebuild --list`
shows three unresolved attempts. One saved system build failed because its
snapshot references the removed `hosts/cf-fv1/services` path; one system test
activation failed after `sudo` timed out waiting for a password; one attempt is
marked `blocked_stale_snapshot`. The guard blocks another activation while
these remain unresolved. No attempt was resumed or discarded. This local
recovery is pending operator review; the implementation and fixture status
above remain complete.

Non-activating Home Manager build (2026-09-29): the activation package builds
with both the detailed attempt guidance and the source removal for
`swayest-workstyle`. The package's generated user units contain no workstyle
service or target-wants link, and Waybar uses `{name}`. The active profile still
points to the older generation, so neither change is live. No activation was
performed.

### [x] BUILD-02 — Tighten background-update apply and stale-candidate behavior

Priority: P1. Type: recovery and concurrency gap.
Files: `hosts/cf-fv1/services.nix`, `home/shell/nix-workflow.fish`,
`docs/development.md`.

Evidence: update-apply replaces the repo's `flake.lock` before activation. A
failed switch leaves the accepted lock and live generations potentially out of
sync. The source comparison has a gap before lock replacement/activation.
`update-review` takes the same blocking flock as a potentially six-hour build.

Implementation:
1. Reuse BUILD-01's transaction state and activation coordination.
2. Preserve the previous lock content and record whether the candidate lock was
   accepted and which activation phases completed. Recovery must not overwrite
   unrelated edits made after failure.
3. Detect source/lock changes immediately before acceptance and fail clearly.
   Coordinate cooperating writers and document the remaining external-edit
   limitation rather than claiming a general filesystem transaction.
4. Make review/status return useful information promptly during an active build,
   rather than waiting for the build lock indefinitely.
5. Explain and test the intentional policy of updating only nixpkgs versus
   updating compatible Home Manager inputs; do not change policy incidentally.

Done when: stale candidates are rejected, review is responsive, failed apply
reports the exact lock/live-state relationship, and no unrelated lock edit is
lost. Include a stubbed concurrent-writer/failure test.

Completed: review returns without waiting on the updater lock; update apply
holds the updater and shared activation locks on separate descriptors, then
rechecks source and lock immediately before acceptance. Transaction resume
checks saved source/lock state, rejects and preserves third-party lock edits,
and reports which update activation phase failed. Theme preference writes now
coordinate with the shared lock. Fixtures covered concurrent source/lock edits,
candidate acceptance, failure reporting, nonblocking review, and the
nixpkgs-only update policy. Independent review passed. Flake check, NixOS build,
and Home Manager activation build passed; the latter used a disposable source
snapshot because the checkout contains an unrelated untracked Fish file. No
live apply was run. External writers that ignore the lock can still race the
atomic lock rename. Commit: `98f06d0`.

### [ ] BOOT-01 — Replace blanket five-second startup deadlines with scoped limits

Priority: P1. Type: configuration risk requiring runtime measurements.
