### [ ] NET-01 — Make network exposure match the documented access boundary

Priority: P1. Type: confirmed broad bindings; intended exposure needs decision.
Files: `hosts/cf-fv1/media.nix`, `hosts/cf-fv1/services.nix`,
`modules/hardening.nix`, `docs/user-parameters.md`.

Initial audit evidence (pre-implementation): most Compose ports used
`HOST:CONTAINER` without a bind address; Emby uses host networking. Samba's
comment said LAN-only but `hosts allow` contained `127. 192.` rather than the
actual LAN CIDR. Shared hardening opened AirVPN port 64480 globally, even
though that is a media-specific value. The source changes below address the UI
bindings, Samba policy, and firewall ownership; remaining ingress and runtime
questions are recorded separately.

Implementation:
1. Inventory listeners and Docker publishing rules read-only. Record which UI
   needs localhost, LAN, or Tailscale access; ask where existing intent is unclear.
2. Introduce explicit bind/subnet settings. Do not blindly bind everything to
   loopback and break remote access, and do not assume the host firewall alone
   constrains Docker-published ports.
3. Scope Samba allow rules to the intended networks and use `mediaMountPoint`
   for its share path instead of hardcoded `/media`.
4. Move media-specific firewall policy into the owning media module. Distinguish
   VPN-provider forwarded ports from ports that actually need LAN publication.

Done when: authorized and unauthorized-interface checks match the documented
matrix, SSH recovery access survives, and all published ports have an owner.

Implementation progress (2026-09-27): Compose web ports bind to the configured
loopback address. The operator confirmed media UIs must remain reachable over
Tailscale, so a systemd unit configures Serve HTTPS endpoints on 8443-8448 to
the loopback backends; the media module owns those Tailscale interface ports and
AirVPN-forwarded port 64480. Shared hardening owns SSH 22. Samba uses the
configured media mount, denies non-loopback clients, and does not open firewall
ports. Flake check and NixOS build pass.

Read-only live check: the running current-system matches the built configuration.
Tailscale Serve is active; all six HTTPS endpoints returned responses with TLS
verification successful (8443: 302, 8444: 401, 8445: 200, 8446: 200, 8447: 403,
8448: 200), and the certificate was issued. Serve listeners bind to Tailscale
addresses. Samba's effective config is loopback-only but smbd binds wildcard
sockets; Emby also binds wildcard port 8096. Same-host requests to the LAN and
Tailscale addresses succeeded for Emby, which does not prove how remote clients
are filtered. Firewall rule inspection was denied to this unprivileged account;
no separate LAN or tailnet client was available for remote probes. NET-01
remains pending those interface-level checks and confirmation that Emby 8096,
Samba, and other direct service ports are blocked remotely while tailnet HTTPS
remains reachable.

Read-only recheck (2026-09-28): the six HTTPS listeners remain bound to the
Tailscale addresses. SSH, Samba ports 139/445, and Emby 8096 listen on wildcard
addresses. One Tailscale peer is online, but no remote probe was made; these
listener observations do not establish firewall filtering or Samba's remote
